The expected risk (we consider as the generalization error bound) and excess risk of $F$ are defined as:

\ifdefined 1
\begin{align*}
    & ~ \text{Expected Risk: }{\cal R}(F) := {\mathbb{E}}_{(X, Y) \sim {\cal D}}[ \| F(X) - Y \|_F^2], \quad \text{Excess Risk: }\Delta {\cal R}(F) := {\cal R}(F) - {\cal R}(F^*).
\end{align*}
\else
\begin{align*}
    & ~ \text{Expected Risk: }{\cal R}(F) := {\mathbb{E}}_{(X, Y) \sim {\cal D}}[ \| F(X) - Y \|_F^2], \quad \text{Excess Risk: }\Delta {\cal R}(F) := {\cal R}(F) - {\cal R}(F^*).
\end{align*}

\fi

An $ N $-layer transformer model is formally defined as:
\ifdefined 1
\begin{align} F(X, \theta) := \varepsilon \cdot F_{(N)}(F_{(N-1)}( \cdots F_{(2)}(F_{(1)}(X + E, \theta), \theta) \cdots ), \theta),
\end{align}
\else
\begin{align} F(X, \theta) := \varepsilon \cdot F_{(N)}(F_{(N-1)}( \cdots F_{(2)}(F_{(1)}(X + E, \theta), \theta) \cdots ), \theta),
\end{align}
\fi
where $ E \in \mathbb{R}^{L \times d} $ is the positional embedding matrix\footnote{We choose $E = {\bf 0}_{L \times d}$ (NoPE, No Positional Embedding, \citep{kpn+23}) or ignore it as a fixed matrix (this can be regarded as a part of the training dataset) in the range of this paper. 
} and $\theta$ is the set of all trainable parameters.

Given the training dataset $\mathbb{D} = \{(X_i, Y_i)\}_{i=1}^n \subset {\cal D}$, we define the overall training objective as:
\begin{align*}
    {\cal L}(t, \mathbb{D}) := {\mathbb{E}}_{ (X, Y) \sim \mathbb{D} } [ \| F(X, \theta(t)) - Y \|_F^2].
\end{align*}

conclusions:
